1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:03,440
This is a lump of corroded bronze,

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:06,240
pulled from a shipwreck in 1901.

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:08,960
Most people think machines with gears like this didn't

4
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:12,240
appear until the great clocks of medieval Europe.

5
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:14,240
But look inside.

6
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:16,000
Thirty gears.

7
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:19,040
Teeth barely a millimetre and a half apart.

8
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:21,040
A machine that could track the Moon,

9
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:25,080
predict eclipses, and keep count of the Olympic Games.

10
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:27,280
And it was built more than 2,000 years

11
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:28,280
ago.

12
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:29,760
So who made it?

13
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:31,760
And how on earth did it work?

14
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:38,720
In the spring of 1900,

15
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:40,880
a crew of sponge divers from the island of

16
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:43,560
Symi was driven off course by a storm.

17
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:46,000
They took shelter at Antikythera,

18
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:49,480
a tiny island between Crete and mainland Greece.

19
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:50,640
When the weather cleared,

20
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:52,000
one of them went down,

21
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:55,600
about 45 metres, and came back up shaken,

22
00:00:55,620 --> 00:00:58,320
talking about bodies on the sea floor.

23
00:00:58,580 --> 00:00:59,920
They weren't bodies.

24
00:00:59,940 --> 00:01:02,720
They were statues, bronze and marble,

25
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:04,960
lying among the remains of a cargo ship that

26
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:07,360
sank around seventy to sixty B.C.

27
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:10,540
Over the following months the divers brought up statues,

28
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:14,220
glassware, pottery, and, most likely in the summer of

29
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:17,820
1901, a shapeless lump of corroded bronze

30
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:19,460
and rotted wood.

31
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:21,340
Next to the statues,

32
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:23,540
nobody gave it a second look.

33
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:25,980
Then, in May 1902,

34
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,260
the archaeologist Valerios Stais noticed something in one of

35
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:30,620
the pieces.

36
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:31,900
A gear wheel.

37
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:35,880
A gear, in a ship that sank more than

38
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:37,160
2,000 years ago,

39
00:01:37,180 --> 00:01:38,760
made no sense.

40
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:41,400
For decades, the fragments sat in the museum in

41
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:44,960
Athens, a puzzle almost nobody could read.

42
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:47,280
The man who refused to let it go was

43
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:50,040
the historian Derek de Solla Price.

44
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:52,120
In the early 1970s,

45
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:55,800
working with the Greek physicist Charalampos Karakalos,

46
00:01:55,820 --> 00:01:57,240
he X-rayed the fragments,

47
00:01:57,260 --> 00:01:59,240
and for the first time people could see gears

48
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:01,080
inside the stone.

49
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:03,640
Then, in 2005,

50
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:06,680
a research team brought an eight-tonne X-ray scanner to

51
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:09,800
the museum, and sliced the fragments into thousands of

52
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:11,480
virtual layers.

53
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:13,960
What they found was astonishing.

54
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:17,800
Thirty surviving gears, cut by hand from sheet bronze,

55
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:19,640
with triangular teeth.

56
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:21,160
And on the surfaces,

57
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:23,720
thousands of tiny Greek letters.

58
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:26,760
The machine had come with its own instruction manual.

59
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:31,200
So, how did it work?

60
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:32,920
Picture a wooden case,

61
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:34,333
about the size of a shoebox,

62
00:02:34,353 --> 00:02:36,320
with a handle on the side.

63
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:39,000
Turn the handle, and a small crown gear drives

64
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:40,960
the biggest wheel inside,

65
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:44,200
a wheel with 223 teeth.

66
00:02:44,380 --> 00:02:47,360
One full turn of that wheel is one year.

67
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:50,000
On the front, a pointer carries the Sun around

68
00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:51,640
a ring of the zodiac,

69
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:53,600
and around that, a ring for the months of

70
00:02:53,620 --> 00:02:55,480
the Egyptian calendar.

71
00:02:55,660 --> 00:02:58,040
A second pointer carries the Moon.

72
00:02:58,140 --> 00:02:59,960
But the Moon goes round the sky more than

73
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:01,160
thirteen times a year,

74
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:03,400
so the gears have to speed it up,

75
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:05,880
and by exactly the right amount.

76
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:08,120
The Greeks knew that the Moon makes two hundred

77
00:03:08,140 --> 00:03:11,920
and fifty-four trips around the zodiac every nineteen years.

78
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:14,013
So the gearing needs a ratio of two hundred

79
00:03:14,033 --> 00:03:16,600
and fifty-four, to nineteen.

80
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:18,680
Here is how they built it.

81
00:03:18,780 --> 00:03:21,440
64 teeth, driving 38.

82
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:23,960
48, driving 24.

83
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:25,240
127,

84
00:03:25,260 --> 00:03:27,000
driving 32.

85
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:29,560
Multiply them together, and you get two hundred and

86
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:31,880
fifty-four over nineteen.

87
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:32,760
Exactly.

88
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:39,260
But the Moon has another trick.

89
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:42,500
It doesn't cross the sky at a steady speed.

90
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:44,940
We now know its orbit is an ellipse,

91
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:47,100
so it speeds up when it's closer to us,

92
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,780
and slows down when it's further away.

93
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,860
The Greek astronomer Hipparchus had built a theory to

94
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:56,300
explain that unevenness, and somebody turned that theory into

95
00:03:56,320 --> 00:03:57,700
bronze.

96
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,300
Two identical gears sit face to face,

97
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:02,620
on slightly different centres.

98
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:04,860
A small pin on one rides in a slot

99
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:06,460
cut into the other.

100
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:08,700
As they turn, the pin slides in and out

101
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:11,220
along the slot, so the second gear is pushed

102
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:14,100
a little ahead, then held a little back.

103
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:17,980
Faster, then slower, once every lunar month.

104
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,260
And because the Moon's orbit itself slowly swings round,

105
00:04:21,280 --> 00:04:24,100
this whole pair sits on top of another rotating

106
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:26,140
gear that carries it all the way round once

107
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:28,660
every 8.88 years.

108
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:31,020
A variable-speed gear train,

109
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:33,180
riding on a moving platform.

110
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:35,260
More than 2,000 years ago.

111
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:40,940
Now turn the box around.

112
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:44,060
The back is covered by two great spirals.

113
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:46,180
The upper one is a calendar of two hundred

114
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:50,220
and thirty-five months, laid out over five turns.

115
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:52,700
It's built on the Metonic cycle:

116
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:55,580
after nineteen years, the phases of the Moon fall

117
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:57,620
on the same dates again.

118
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:00,740
As you crank, the pointer works its way around

119
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:04,140
and outwards, with a little pin following the groove,

120
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:07,140
so you always know which turn you're on.

121
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:10,900
Inside it sits a small dial divided into four.

122
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:13,420
It keeps track of the great athletic games of

123
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:15,580
Greece, including the Olympics,

124
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:17,660
and it's the only pointer on the whole machine

125
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:19,700
that turns the other way.

126
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,980
The lower spiral is the one that mattered most.

127
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:24,860
223 months,

128
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:26,620
over four turns.

129
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:29,020
This is the Saros cycle.

130
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:32,540
Babylonian astronomers had noticed that eclipses repeat in a

131
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:35,060
pattern about eighteen years long.

132
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:37,180
So here, the months with an eclipse are marked

133
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:38,980
with a tiny symbol:

134
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:40,540
38 for the Moon,

135
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:42,380
27 for the Sun,

136
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:44,580
some even giving the hour.

137
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:46,780
Turn the handle, follow the pointer,

138
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:49,340
and you could see an eclipse coming years ahead.

139
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:56,220
And then there's the part we're still arguing about.

140
00:05:56,360 --> 00:05:58,980
The writing on the front names the planets:

141
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,940
Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter,

142
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:03,300
and Saturn.

143
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:06,420
But whatever gears drove them have been lost.

144
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:08,420
Then, in 2016,

145
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:12,260
the scans revealed two numbers hidden in that text.

146
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:13,940
462,

147
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:15,220
for Venus.

148
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:16,580
442,

149
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:17,940
for Saturn.

150
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:22,020
Planetary cycles, and remarkably accurate ones.

151
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:25,860
In 2021, a team at University College London

152
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:28,340
used those numbers to design gear trains that would

153
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:30,780
actually fit in the space available:

154
00:06:30,840 --> 00:06:32,740
a reconstruction with the Sun,

155
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:35,060
the Moon and all five planets,

156
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:37,780
moving on rings around the front dial.

157
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:39,900
The team is clear that they can't be certain

158
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:40,980
it's right.

159
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,300
Nobody has found those gears to check.

160
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:44,660
But if they're even close,

161
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:46,700
this little box was a model of the whole

162
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:47,780
known cosmos.

163
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:52,780
So who built it?

164
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:54,260
We don't know.

165
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:56,220
The names of the months on the back point

166
00:06:56,240 --> 00:06:58,260
to Corinth, or one of its colonies,

167
00:06:58,280 --> 00:07:01,220
perhaps Syracuse, the city of Archimedes,

168
00:07:01,240 --> 00:07:03,220
who had died more than a century before the

169
00:07:03,240 --> 00:07:04,820
ship went down.

170
00:07:05,080 --> 00:07:08,140
The Roman writer Cicero describes machines that showed the

171
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:09,540
motions of the Sun,

172
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:11,500
the Moon and the planets,

173
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:14,620
one made by Archimedes and another by the philosopher

174
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:16,180
Posidonius.

175
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:18,140
Probably not this one.

176
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:20,740
But a sign that it wasn't the only one.

177
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,660
What we do know is what came after.

178
00:07:23,720 --> 00:07:24,860
Nothing.

179
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:26,980
For well over a thousand years,

180
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,260
nothing with gearing this complex survives anywhere.

181
00:07:30,360 --> 00:07:33,140
The next machines that come close are the astronomical

182
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:35,700
clocks of fourteenth-century Europe.

183
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:37,860
The knowledge didn't just fade.

184
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:39,140
It vanished.

185
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:41,460
And we only know it ever existed because one

186
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:44,660
ship sank, in exactly the right place.

187
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:50,340
Today, what's left is 82 fragments,

188
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:52,340
in a museum in Athens.

189
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:54,980
But the idea inside them came back.

190
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:57,460
Gears that turn the movements of the sky into

191
00:07:57,480 --> 00:07:59,420
the movement of a pointer.

192
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:02,180
It came back in those medieval clocks.

193
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:03,860
Then in pocket watches.

194
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:06,580
And today it's ticking on millions of wrists,

195
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:08,980
in machines that still keep time with nothing but

196
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,420
gears and springs, and no battery at all.

197
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:14,980
The builder of this box would understand one of

198
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:16,660
them instantly.

199
00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:18,360
So let's open one up.

